well my name is Jeff Friedman I'm a  professor at the Howard Hughes Medical  Institute at Rockefeller University and  I'm pleased to be part of the IEEE  biology series today I'm going to tell  you about our research into the causes  of obesity and the discovery of the  hormone leptin the clinical problem our  science addresses is obesity and at some  level we'd like to understand why it is  some individuals have the appearance of  the child on the left and other people  have the appearance of the child on the  right if you ask the average person why  the difference most people would say  well the individual on the Left eats too  much well that would be true but it  would ignore the deeper question which  is why does the individual on the Left  eat too much and I'd like to try to  answer that question for you today now  there are several general reasons to  explain why some individuals might eat  more than others one possibility is to  suggest that the obese lack a level of  willpower that's more evident in lean  people I find that point of view is more  often favored by lean people second  possibility is to suggest that we live  in a modern environment that predisposes  to obesity and a third possibility would  be to suggest that body weight is  controlled by biological factors and  genes the interest of time I'm not going  to discuss the relative contributions of  willpower and environment but tell you  in fact that there's substantial  evidence that genes play a dominant role  to control body weight there are a  number of ways to assess the genetic  contributions to a particular trait the  standard way to do so is to  systematically compare twin pairs  identical twins share all of their genes  fraternal twins only share half their  genes  so if identical twins are more alike for  a particular trait than non identical  twins we can calculate the genetic  contribution to that trait if you do  this for obesity what you find is that  obesity is as or more hereditable than  any trait that gets that's yet been  studied with the exception of height in  fact obesity is more hereditable than  many traits that we commonly consider  have a genetic basis including  schizophrenia diabetes hypertension  alcoholism and heart disease  in fact we can calculate based on this  about that about seventy to eighty  percent of the variation in body weight  can be ascribed to genetic factors now  to show you the potency of these genetic  factors to control weight I would like  to tell you about a case report this is  the child whose image I began by showing  you the child was of normal weight at  birth but began to develop morbid  obesity very early in life  his parents reported intense hyperphagia  or an intense increased appetite to the  point where they even padlocked the  refrigerator to keep the child from  getting free access to food despite this  to cut the child kept getting fatter and  at the age of four he weighed 90 pounds  and was already pre-diabetic now this  child had a similarly affected  eight-year-old cousin who weighed over  200 pounds  both these children came from an inbred  family and oftentimes when you see in an  extreme phenotype in an inbred family in  this case as a result of a first cousin  marriage it's a result of a genetic  alteration so Steve O'Reilly and  colleagues suggested that perhaps this  child was obese as a result of a  mutation in a particular gene and they  hypothesized that this child might be  missing a hormone leptin that was first  identified by my laboratory leptin is a  hormone made by fat tissue that's  secreted into the blood where it  circulates ultimately acting on the  brain to regulate food intake in the  absence of leptin animals eat more and  so a rally suggests that perhaps this  child was missing the hormone leptin  they tested and indeed the child was  missing leptin so they decided to now  take leptin that was made in the  laboratory and give it back to this  child and what you can see is that the  child from between the ages of 4 &amp; 6  lost a substantial amount of weight  despite getting taller in addition the  child ate less before the first leptin  injection he ate 2000 calories at a  single meal after a few left and  injections the child now ate 180  calories at the same meal which is the  appropriate number of calories for a  child of that age and size in this case  the child was obese because of a defect  in the hormone leptin without leptin no  signal is ever generated that there  - quit fat stores this is a stimulus to  eat more and this child kept eating kept  getting fatter without ever making the  signal that shut the system off and now  here's a picture of the child at the age  of 8 it's the same child I began by  showing you a picture of so this child  was obese not as a result of a lack of  willpower or a toxic environment this  child was obese because a lack of a lack  of the hormone leptin studies of this  child and other individuals has now  suggested that body weight is regulated  by a hormonal loop of which leptin is an  important component at our stable weight  food intake equals energy expenditure  and we make a particular amount of  leptin now imagine if that mass falls  after a diet leptin level would fall a  low leptin level will be provide a  stimulus to eat more much is the same as  the case in the untreated leptin  deficient child and you'll now an  individual have a potent stimulus to  their appetite until such time as their  fat mass and leptin level returns to the  starting point conversely if you overeat  for a period of time a bit binge of  eating fat mass goes up and leptin level  goes up with it  this will suppress appetite again until  such time as fat mass and leptin level  return to the starting point  so because leptin is made in proportion  to fat mass it provides a signal that  can then control food and take in the  response to weight loss or weight gain  and what this system does in a sense is  set up a biological response that  resists weight change in either  direction if you lose fat mass leptin  level falls and it's a stimulus to gain  weight if you gain fat mass leptin level  rises and that suppresses appetite and  by this mechanism body weight can be  maintained within a relatively narrow  range now if you hear all of this and  find that there was an undiscovered  hormone circulating in the in the blood  of animals and humans it seems rather  mysterious to consider how this hormone  was identified so I'd like to now tell  you about how I became interested in  this hormone and how it was discovered  my interest in this area began with an  interest in behavior how molecules  control behavior and my first interest  centered on a  another hormone that said to regulate  appetite CCK cholecystokinin is a  hormone made by an endocrine cells in  your intestine in response to food when  food is present inside your gut CCK is  secreted into the blood and it  stimulates the contraction of your  gallbladder and the secretion of enzymes  by your pancreas that aid in digestion  and this was the situation in 1968 prior  to the work of Smith and Gibbs who also  showed that CCK can suppress appetite  they suggested in fact that this at this  intestinal hormone when it secreted not  only helps you a digestion but also  circulates in the blood and acts on the  brain to shut off a meal made perfect  sense excitement in this area was  amplified when a few years later C CK  was also found in the brain by a group  led by Graham da Cray this then  suggested that perhaps appetite and body  weight are regulated by a comp by a  hormone that is expressed in both the  gut and the brain and that the levels of  perla Freesat peripherally synthesized  in brain synthesize C CK might control  food intake and body weight and this was  referred to at the time as the gut brain  axis interest in this area was amplified  even further in 1979 when Strauss niala  reported that the levels of C CK were  reduced in the brains of a strange  genetically obese strain of mouse known  as the OB mouse the OB Mouse is obese as  a result of a defect in a single gene  and yellow and strauss suggested that  that defective gene might be C CK this  endogenous appetite suppressant with a  lower level of the endogenous appetite  suppressant the animals might eat more  well this is a picture of the OB mouse  it's the the animal on the far right is  an OB Mouse and the animal  here a second to the firm from the right  is a lean litter made of that animal the  only defect between the OB mouse and his  sibling littermate is a defect in a  single gene that single genetic defect  leads the OB animal to eat more move  around less ultimately weighing three  times as much and having five times as  much fat as the sibling littermate and  as I mentioned yellow suggested that  perhaps this animal overrate because it  was missing CCK this endogenous appetite  suppressant the only problem with this  idea is that it very well might be wrong  and this had to do with the fact that a  person I was working with at the time  Bruce Schneider published the  contemporaneous report showing that the  levels of CCK in brain were no different  in OB mice versus their lean littermates  and so we and others wondered whether or  not CCK was the defective gene in the OB  animals well one of the first things we  did at the time was to clone the CCK  gene and the idea was to clone the CCK  gene and see where on the chromosome it  mapped whether it mapped near the  location of the OB gene we quickly found  that C CK mapped to the distal end of  chromosome 9 this is a map of chromosome  9 with a variety of genes that map there  the only problem was that the OB gene  did map to chromosome 9 in map to  chromosome 6 so these data excluded the  possibility that C CK was the OB gene  and raised the question as to well if  it's not C CK what is it in the 1980s  when we did this work a new technology  was emerging that allowed one to  identify defective genes not based on a  knowledge of their function but simply  based on a detailed knowledge of the  chromosomal location of that gene this  methodology was known as positional  cloning and we set out then to identify  the OB gene using positional cloning  this required identifying genes in the  region of the chromosome where OB was  known to map and one day on a Friday we  found a gene in the region of OB that  was expressed only in fat tissue this is  a northern blot it's away at the time of  measuring RNA levels and only fat tissue  had an RNA for this gene which we knew  map near the OB gene the next step was  to ask is this gene defective in strains  of mice that are genetically obese and  indeed it was if you look here at Lane 2  that's fat tissue from a normal Mouse  Lane 3 is fat tissue from an OB mouse of  the same strain that that strain fails  to make the OB RNA and that's why it's  bad Lane 4 is RNA from fat tissue of  different strain of mouse and lane five  is RNA from the obese animal from that  same strain in this case increased  levels of RNA are produced and that's  because the RNA produces a defective  protein the animal then makes more and  more RNA and as a means of trying to  compensate unsuccessfully because the  RNA makes a defective protein  well the demonstration that this gene  was defective in two different OB  strains of mice confirmed that we in  fact could identify the defective gene  and this was published to some fanfare  in 1994 with this image of an obese  Mouse out weighing two of its sibling  littermates shortly after identifying  the OB gene subsequent studies revealed  in fact that the defective gene encoded  this hormone leptin it's the hormone  made by fat tissue that regulates  appetite by acting by on brain centers  one of the key experiments was done with  collaborators Jeff Hollis Kate and gaggi  wall and Steven burly together we showed  that if you inject saline into an OB  animal nothing happens they stay obese  but if you inject leptin made in the  laboratory animals normalize their  phenotype much the same as a rally and  colleagues later showed for this leptin  deficient child now leptin mutations are  quite rare there are only a few dozen  such mutations in the world which is  characteristic of hormones which are  poor targets for mutation but we now  know that perhaps 10 to 15% of morbid  obesity as a result of single gene  defects in other genes in the neural  circuit on which leptin acts and so this  has provided a great stimulus into obese  into the research into the causes of  obesity and tells us now that obesity is  very often a result of specific genetic  defects I suspect that will identify  ever more of these defects now with  advances in DNA sequencing and other new  technologies and so I think research in  this area has been greatly stimulated by  the identification of leptin and the  realization that obesity is can be the  result of genetic defects in a neural  circuit that regulates food intake and  body weight  you

